<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
        Required for sidecar yt-dlp (PyInstaller bundle): yt-dlp extracts its
        embedded Python framework to /tmp/_MEI*/ at runtime and dlopen()s it.
        That framework was code-signed by yt-dlp's publisher (Team ID different
        from ours), so under hardened runtime the loader rejects it with
        "mapping process and mapped file have different Team IDs". This
        entitlement relaxes that specific rule for our app.

        Notarization: still required, Apple allows this entitlement for
        Developer ID distribution. Library Validation off ≠ unsigned code OK;
        the libraries themselves still need valid signatures.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
        Required for the sidecar `node` (V8), which yt-dlp spawns via its
        js-runtimes flag (node:<path>) to solve YouTube's n-challenge. Without
        these, macOS downloads fail with:

        NOTE: never write two adjacent hyphens in this comment. Apple's
        entitlements parser (AMFIUnserializeXML) is a strict XML parser and
        rejects that sequence inside a comment ("syntax error"), which fails
        codesign for the WHOLE app. That is why the flag above is written
        without its leading dashes.

          [jsc] Error solving n challenge ... using "node" provider:
                Error running node process (returncode: -5)
          ERROR: [youtube] <id>: No video formats found!

        returncode -5 = SIGTRAP: the kernel killed node inside V8's
        SetPermissions when it tried to make JIT memory executable.

        Why our build strips node's own entitlements: notarization requires
        every executable inside the .app to be signed with OUR identity, so
        Tauri re-signs each sidecar with hardened runtime + THIS file —
        replacing the entitlements the official nodejs.org binary ships with
        (which include both keys below). We must re-grant them.

        allow-jit alone is not enough on recent macOS — V8 also needs
        allow-unsigned-executable-memory. Both are permitted for Developer ID
        distribution and notarization (Electron/Node apps ship them routinely).

        Windows is unaffected: no hardened runtime, no entitlements.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
